# PARSeg Experiment Summary

> HISTORICAL RAW ARCHIVE — frozen after the PARSeg/early OffSeg exploration.
> It mixes final values, interim readings, predictions and assistant-written
> interpretations. It is retained for traceability, not as the current route or
> a source that can be quoted without checking. Canonical status and verified
> result labels live in `THESIS_ROUTE.md` and `EXPERIMENTS.md`.

## Baseline

- Current ADE20K PARSeg3/base reference used in recent tests: about `48.17` to `48.2` mIoU.
- FT runs load this base: `work_dirs/parseg3_ade20k_160k-512x512_4x4_try1/iter_160000.pth`.
- Earlier discussion also mentioned a stronger PARSeg3 asset around `48.84`; keep this separate from the current `try1` baseline.
- Validation/checkpoint interval was shortened for experiments:
  - ADE20K / Cityscapes: every `8000` iterations.
  - COCO-Stuff: every `4000` iterations.

## Diagnostic Findings

### Active-class oracle

- ADE train subset, `2000` images:
  - baseline mIoU: `48.17`
  - active-class oracle: `58.39`
  - ceiling delta: `+10.22`
- Interpretation: knowing which classes are present has a large oracle ceiling, but this does not make a learnable active-class predictor useful.

### Learnable active-class / presence predictor

- Realizable gain was only about `+0.03`.
- Interpretation: active-class prediction from the current base feature/output is not a reliable main direction.

### Error confidence and type

- Wrong pixels include many confident errors:
  - confidence >= 0.7: `54.7%`
  - confidence >= 0.9: `28.6%`
- Error decomposition:
  - ABSENT-FP: `42.6%`
  - PRESENT-CONF: `57.4%`
- Interpretation: the main issue is not simply uncertain boundary pixels or missing active-class priors; many mistakes are confident co-present class confusions.

### Top-k / rerank oracle

- On wrong pixels, GT often already appears in the candidate list:
  - top-2: `54.8%`
  - top-3: `71.8%`
  - top-5: `84.5%`
- A top-k rerank oracle showed a very large upper bound; top-2 rerank was discussed as roughly `+18.38`.
- Interpretation: the information is often already present in the logits, but the final ranking/decision is wrong.

### Boundary / interior probe

- Error location split: present-conf errors are about `63.7%` interior / `36.3%` boundary; about `26%` of all errors hug a boundary.
- Boundary vs interior ORACLE (`tools/probe_boundary_oracle.py`, ADE val, `2000` images, base `48.17`), perfect fix of each region:
  - boundary `r=3`: mIoU `59.68`  (delta `+11.52`, band = `13.2%` of px)
  - boundary `r=5`: mIoU `64.58`  (delta `+16.41`, band = `20.6%` of px)
  - boundary `r=8`: mIoU `70.16`  (delta `+21.99`, band = `30.2%` of px)
  - interior (non-boundary wrong px, `r=5`): mIoU `76.90`  (delta `+28.74`)
- Interpretation: the boundary route has a real but capped oracle ceiling (`+16.41` at `r=5`) and the realized gain from it (IGR) was ~0; the larger recoverable pool is interior/semantic (`+28.74`), which the decision-side family could not reach either. High-resolution boundary-only refinement is not the main ADE20K bottleneck.

### Frozen feature separability

- Frozen PARSeg3 `align` features linearly separate top confusion pairs at roughly `98% - 100%`.
- Top confusions cluster around the wall family (ceiling/door/window/cabinet/mirror/curtain <-> wall), plus building/tree, road/sidewalk, rug/floor, armchair/sofa; top-25 pairs cover ~`33.7%`.
- Interpretation: the feature contains separable information; the problem is how the feature is converted into decisions/logits.

## Model Attempts and Results

### SCA2 / GEO

- ADE interim numbers:
  - GEO at `152000 iter`: about `47.34`
  - SCA2 at `144000 iter`: about `46.49`
  - both below base `48.2`
- GEO on Cityscapes had not clearly fallen behind at that time, but ADE results were not supportive.
- Decision: stop as main ADE direction.

### PARSeg6 family

- TCQ task token degenerates in semantic-only training because the task token is a constant parameter.
- Evidence fusion was not independent: offset logits were both used to find hard pixels and fused into the final output.
- Scaffold tests were mostly AST/string checks and did not run forward.
- Configs inherited dead parseg3 parameters, making reproduction confusing.
- Decision: too much module stacking and not a coherent main line.

### CDC

- PARSeg3 plus a residual cosine decision head.
- Identity-safe and useful as a pilot.
- Contribution too small for the main thesis direction.

### CAS

- Tried to keep PAL attributes while adding confusion-aware attribute separation.
- No recorded positive mIoU signal.
- Decision: not used as main line without independent positive evidence.

### APC

- Inspired by SSA-Seg adaptive classifier, but using feature purity instead of mask confidence.
- Revised with GT-EMA centers, cosine logits + scale, bounded gate, and coarse centering.
- FT results:
  - `8000 iter`: `45.07`
  - `136000 iter`: `46.83`  (still about `1` point below the contemporaneous base)
- Interpretation: regressed throughout and never reached base; the aux / teacher / distill gradients most likely disturbed the tuned PARSeg3 decision surface (the bounded gate blocks the forward path but not the gradient).
- Decision: stop.

### IGR

- Frozen PARSeg3 plus image-guided high-resolution point recompute (image-guided PointRend).
- FT results:
  - `8000 iter`: `48.08`
  - `16000 iter`: `48.07`
  - later: oscillates around `48.05` (no gain, a touch below base).
- Why it cannot gain: the point head re-classifies uncertain points from the SAME frozen features (image high-freq gives boundary location, not class), so it reproduces base on ties; uncertainty sampling structurally misses the confident INTERIOR errors; and mIoU barely rewards a thin boundary band.
- Decision: negative ablation.

### GDS

- Kept PARSeg3/PAL/AGCF and added an attribute-geometry residual branch.
- FT behavior:
  - final validation stayed around `48.1700`, same as base.
  - checkpoint: `decode_head.gds_alpha alpha = -0.2341673821`
  - corresponding `gate = -0.0459958166`
- Interpretation: the branch trained and the gate moved, but the global signed residual became negative and did not improve mIoU.
- Decision: global residual correction is the wrong interface.

### SGC

- Replaced GDS global signed gate with a positive local spatial selector.
- FT results:
  - `8000 iter`: `48.03`
  - `16000 iter`: `48.05`
- Interpretation: even local selection did not find useful corrections.
- Decision: stop the residual correction / auxiliary classifier / selector family.

### PALX

- Internal PAL refinement change rather than external correction.
- Replaces PARSeg3 `prototype_attribute_refinement` with PALX refinement.
- Final prediction still follows the native PARSeg3 path:
  - `base_head_logits`
  - `refinement_head_logits`
  - `AGCF`
- Adds training-time PAL geometry losses:
  - GT center alignment.
  - base-mined hard-negative margin.
- Local implementation:
  - commit: `13c6528 feat: add PARSeg PALX head`
- FT results:
  - `8000 iter`: `48.0300`
  - `16000 iter`: `48.3100`
  - `24000 iter`: `48.0300`
  - `32000 iter`: `48.0300`
- Interpretation: PALX is the first post-failure direction with a real positive validation point (`16k`, about `+0.14` over the current `48.17` base), but the gain is not stable. This suggests a short-window effect or over-training of the frozen-base PAL refinement head, not a full-run-ready method yet.
- Decision: keep `iter_16000.pth` for diagnosis; stop the current unchanged FT after the drop. Next test should narrow the schedule/loss around the early optimum rather than continue to 40k or launch full 160k.
- Follow-up config:
  - `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parsegpalx_ft_short_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`
  - `max_iters=24000`
  - `val_interval=4000`
  - lower geometry weights: `palx_marginw=0.06`, `palx_centerw=0.04`, `palx_margin=0.08`
  - optimizer no longer inherits the 10x head lr; uses AdamW `lr=1e-4`
- Follow-up goal: test whether the `16k` peak is reproducible and stable around `12k/16k/20k` without pushing PAL geometry until collapse.
- Short follow-up observed first three validation points:
  - first val: `48.26`
  - second val: `48.10`
  - third val: `48.07`
- Updated interpretation: the positive PALX signal moved earlier but still decayed quickly. Even the best recorded point (`48.31`) is only about `+0.1` to `+0.14` over the current `48.17/48.2` base and is not enough as a thesis-level model contribution. PALX should not be polished further as the main line unless it produces a reproducible `>= +0.5` mIoU effect.

### EVF (DINOv2 evidence fusion)

- Idea: fuse a second frozen backbone (DINOv2, via timm) into PARSeg3 through a learned per-pixel gate; only the DINOv2 head + gate train.
- Status: REJECTED by design, not run. It relies on an external pretrained model / distillation-style fusion, which is off-target for this thesis: new information must come from re-processing the EXISTING backbone, not from importing a bigger external net. This is a scope decision, not a measured negative.
- Files exist locally (`PARSegEVF.py`, `parseg_evf_*` config) but should be treated as dead unless the scope changes.

### RCR (region-centric re-decision)

- Idea: discover per-image regions by slot-attention over PARSeg3's frozen fused feature, decide each region's class at the REGION level (with inter-region context), then gate-override the base. Motivated by the diagnosis: the confused pairs are ~98% separable in the frozen feature, the errors are confident WHOLE regions, and per-pixel CE under-trains those few regions -> move the unit of decision from pixel to region so each mislabeled region becomes one strong sample.
- Difference from the senior's mask2former / PAL attribute decoder: those use FIXED learned per-class queries (same every image); RCR uses DATA-DEPENDENT regions discovered per image.
- Files: `mmseg/models/decode_heads/PARSegRCR.py`, config `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parseg_rcr_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`.
- FT result: `8000 iter` did not beat base, then degraded monotonically with more training (collapse) -- WORSE than the flat post-hoc attempts.
- Why it collapses: the override content is a coarse region map (100 soft slots over a whole image, grouped at 1/8 and bilinearly upsampled), so any region that mixes classes assigns one wrong class to most of its pixels. The aux loss forces this region map to exist, and CE on `final` pushes the gate `g` up wherever the region map happens to help on a batch; the conv gate then generalizes that rise broadly, so the harmful coarse map increasingly overrides good base pixels -> monotonic decline. Slot attention on frozen features under CE (not reconstruction) is also collapse-prone.
- Decision: confirmed negative, and the strongest evidence yet that the post-hoc gated-override family is exhausted (GDS/SGC/IGR flat, RCR collapses).

## Current Strategic Conclusion

Mostly ruled out:

- active-class predictor
- high-resolution boundary-only refinement
- post-PARSeg residual correction
- external auxiliary classifier / selector
- external pretrained-model fusion / distillation (off-target by scope)
- broad module stacking without a single hypothesis

Most plausible remaining line after DGM:

- Do not correct after PARSeg3 has already produced final logits.
- Do not add another global auxiliary classifier or metric branch.
- If continuing model work, the next hypothesis must be conditional/local: the probe separability is pair/subspace-specific, while DGM used one global 150-way geometry.
- Any new model must explain how it selects the relevant comparison context without using an oracle class set.

### DGM (decision geometry modeling)

- New hypothesis after PALX proved too small: the useful separability already exists in `align` features, but the base/PAL decision surface is not constrained to use a clean feature-class geometry.
- DGM adds a normalized metric classifier inside the PARSeg3 base decision path:
  - raw offset logits are still computed.
  - metric logits are mixed into `base_head_logits` before PAL refinement.
  - PAL refinement and AGCF consume this geometry-aware base, so this is not a final-logit post-hoc correction.
- DGM trains the metric space with:
  - metric auxiliary CE.
  - nearest-negative feature margin.
  - feature-to-class-weight pull.
  - small class-weight separation penalty.
- Configs:
  - full: `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parsegdgm_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`
  - warm-start FT: `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parsegdgm_ft_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`
- Test discipline: run warm-start FT first. If it cannot create a stable positive trend over the `48.17/48.2` checkpoint, do not spend a full 160k run on DGM.
- Warm-start FT results:
  - `8000 iter`: `47.64`
  - `16000 iter`: `48.10`
  - `24000 iter`: `48.14`
  - `32000 iter`: `48.16`
  - `40000 iter`: `48.10`
- Interpretation: DGM recovered from the early hit but never exceeded the PARSeg3 checkpoint (`48.17/48.2`). The normalized global metric classifier does not turn the probe-observed separability into validation mIoU. Do not run full 160k.

### LCR (local candidate relation)

- New hypothesis after DGM failed: probe separability is local and conditional, not captured by one global 150-way metric geometry.
- LCR builds a per-pixel candidate set from raw PARSeg3 base logits, scores only those candidate/class relations against the aligned feature, and injects the learned delta into `base_head_logits` before PAL refinement.
- This is not a fixed easy-confusion table and not an oracle active-class set: the comparison context is selected dynamically by the model's own current candidate list.
- Training target:
  - auxiliary CE on relation-corrected base logits.
  - candidate ranking loss only where GT appears in the local top-k, directly targeting wrong top-k ordering.
- Configs:
  - full: `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parseglcr_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`
  - warm-start FT: `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parseglcr_ft_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`
- Stop rule: run warm-start FT first. If `16k` is not clearly above `48.17/48.2`, stop. Continue only if candidate ranking gives a stable positive validation trend.
- Warm-start FT results:
  - `8000 iter`: `48.14`
  - `16000 iter`: `48.01`
  - `24000 iter`: `47.82`
  - `32000 iter`: `48.33`
  - `40000 iter`: `47.87`
- Interpretation: LCR produced one positive checkpoint (`32k`, about `+0.13` to `+0.16` over the current PARSeg3 checkpoint), but the curve is unstable and immediately collapses again. This is stronger than a flat failure, because conditional/local relation can briefly help, but it is still far below a thesis-level stable gain. Do not run full 160k unchanged.

Resolved sequence after RCR:

- RCR did not clear `48.2`: it failed to beat base at `8k` and then collapsed. Per the pre-registered fork, the post-hoc gated-override family is confirmed exhausted (GDS/SGC/IGR flat, RCR collapses).
- PALX briefly reached `48.31` but was not stable.
- DGM failed as a global metric-geometry branch.
- LCR briefly reached `48.33`, suggesting the local candidate-relation hypothesis has a weak signal, but the optimization/window is unstable.
- Meta: every warm-start add-on around the frozen/tuned `48.2` PARSeg3 checkpoint has either stayed near base, collapsed, or produced only a narrow `+0.1` spike. The next step should be diagnosis around the positive spike, not another blind module.

## Next Full-Training Slots

### PLCR (PAL-guided local candidate relation)

- Purpose: combine the two weak positive signals:
  - PALX says PAL attribute geometry can slightly help.
  - LCR says local candidate relation can briefly help.
- Difference from LCR: candidate class representations come from PAL class features, not a standalone class embedding.
- Hypothesis: local top-k comparisons become more stable when the comparison units are PARSeg/PAL attribute prototypes rather than an external relation MLP's learned embeddings.
- Config: `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parsegplcr_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`

### CDR (candidate decision ranking)

- Purpose: isolate the training objective from model structure.
- Difference from LCR/PLCR: no new inference branch and no new logits correction. It keeps native PARSeg3 inference and only adds top-k ranking losses during training.
- Hypothesis: if the oracle/top-k diagnosis is trainable, a direct candidate ranking objective should improve PARSeg3 without extra decoder complexity.
- Config: `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parsegcdr_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`

### OSC (omni-scale context calibration)

- Purpose: test a decoder direction orthogonal to candidate ranking.
- Inspiration: recent strong segmentation decoders repeatedly emphasize multi-scale context and scale selection rather than only logits reranking.
- Mechanism: local, dilated, and global context branches are mixed by a learned scale gate, then injected before offset learning and PAL refinement.
- Hypothesis: some ADE failures come from missing scene/context evidence before decision formation, not merely wrong top-k ordering.
- Config: `local_configs/offseg2/Base/parsegosc_ade20k_160k-512x512.py`

## Stop Rules for New FT Runs

- Load PARSeg3 `iter_160000.pth` for FT sanity checks.
- Initial / 8k validation should be close to `48.17` / `48.2`.
- If `8k < 48.0`, stop.
- If `16k` is still at or below `48.17` with no upward trend, stop.
- Only continue to `40k` or launch full `160k` if `16k` shows a clear positive signal, or if high-confidence co-present confusions improve materially.

## CGR probe (confusion-group regional re-decision)

- User-level decision after reviewing PLCR/CDR/OSC:
  - PALX/PLCR is brittle, heavy, and hard to describe.
  - OSC has the weakest argument.
  - CDR changes too little.
  - These should not occupy the next main training slots unchanged.
- New hypothesis: most failed variants were pixel-independent corrections learned from PARSeg3's own logits/features/confidence. The only direction not fully killed is region/group-level re-decision, but RCR failed because the region/slot map and learned output gate were poor.
- CGR probe is deliberately training-free:
  - fit pure class prototypes from GT-labelled train pixels in PARSeg3 refinement feature space;
  - do not use PARSeg3 confidence or `p_base * confidence` when building prototypes;
  - route pixels only by base top-k group membership, then re-decide within a small confusion group;
  - smooth group scores by feature affinity so replacements are spatially coherent;
  - report baseline mIoU, CGR mIoU, correction, damage, and corr/damage.
- Tool added: `tools/probe_cgr_redecision.py`.
- Main command to run:
  `python tools/probe_cgr_redecision.py local_configs/offseg2/Base/parseg3_ade20k_160k-512x512.py work_dirs/parseg3_ade20k_160k-512x512_4x4_try1/iter_160000.pth --fit-images 2000 --eval-images 2000 --num-prototypes 3 --prototype-cache work_dirs/cgr_parseg3_ade20k_k3_train2000.pt`
- Readout rule:
  - `delta >= +0.20`: worth writing a CGR training head.
  - `+0.05 <= delta < +0.20` with `corr/damage > 1`: write only a light gated variant, not a heavy decoder.
  - otherwise: do not write a full CGR model; inspect group damage and rethink the premise.

### CGR probe results so far

- Fixed-size but over-broad routing (`top-k` intersects group):
  - baseline `47.0143`, CGR `25.6049`, delta `-21.4093`, changed `27.313%`, corr/damage `0.140`.
  - Diagnosis: routing touched far too many pixels; it was effectively rewriting common classes.
- Label-aligned fixed pipeline + narrow routing (`trigger_topk=2`, `min_group_hits=2`):
  - baseline `46.7464`, CGR `46.4445`, delta `-0.3019`, changed `3.123%`, corr/damage `0.395`.
  - Diagnosis: routing width is now sane, but direct prototype winner still damages more than it corrects.
- Next probe change:
  - add `--sweep-margins` and per-group correction/damage reporting.
  - Goal: determine whether high prototype margin or a specific confusion group has positive signal; if every margin/group remains `corr/damage < 1`, do not write a CGR model.
  - Final sweep: corr/damage stayed ~0.139 across margins/groups -> CGR closed, no training head written.

## From-scratch 160k round 1 (four configs)

- LCR : 48.60 at the FINAL iter (peak = endpoint; best local number to date).
- HCE : 47.78. LAR-A: 47.69. Both below base; closed.
- SDR : 48.08 at 120k, then oscillated 47-48; below base at end. Closed.
  Post-mortem: rival/absent margins sharpen logits and shift the entropy
  statistics AGCF's gate depends on; GT-pooled teacher is self-trivial so
  KD ~ sharper CE. Repositioned as the "losses-alone do not work" negative
  ablation supporting LCR's structural necessity.
- LCR 160k->200k continuation at lr=1e-6 (eta_min=1e-8, 40k): no ckpt beat
  48.60. Expected and uninformative (poly had annealed; fresh low-lr restart
  explores nothing). Do not count as evidence about LCR headroom.

## LCR autopsy (2000 val images, same pipeline on both ckpts)

- Learned gate = 0.107 (31% of the 0.35 ceiling): ceiling not binding; the
  GLOBAL scalar is the coarse part.
- Error decomposition: total err 18.05% -> 17.51%. ABSENT-FP 7.69 -> 7.27
  (~78% of the gain); PRESENT-CONF 10.36 -> 10.24 (~22%). LCR mostly
  suppresses absent-class intruders, interior semantic confusion largely
  untouched.
- Interior/boundary split unchanged (63.7/36.3 -> 63.6/36.4). Margin
  histogram unchanged. Top-2 rerank oracle AFTER LCR still +18.98 (pool not
  depleted). Note: top-k recall is computed on each model's OWN wrong
  pixels; do not compare across models.
- Overall-confidence rose ([0.95,1) bucket 289M -> 295M px).

## LCR2 (windowed evidence + conditional per-pixel gate): FAILED

- From-scratch 160k: 47.45 at 80k (above same-iter reference), then decayed;
  <47 until ~120k; 47.71 at 128k; 47.75 at 152k. ~0.85 below LCR v1.
- Led early, lost the consolidation phase. Two candidate mechanisms:
  (a) gate/scorer inputs are base-probability statistics that saturate as
  the model sharpens late in training (v1's input-free scalar gate is
  immune); (b) 13x13 window support de-noises an immature base early but
  erodes boundaries/thin classes once the base is clean. Closed; do not
  iterate. Pre-registered kill rule (96k-128k below v1 curve) would have
  saved the last 40k -- apply kill rules at the gate next time.

## Text-anchor round (three from-scratch 160k slots): geometry axis DEAD

- Principle for all text work: GT-derived text allowed in TRAINING only;
  inference must be text-free (contrast with DTFormer, where GT-mapped
  vocabulary at inference = presence oracle; our measured +10.22 axis).
- Key information argument (confirmed): per-image GT text is redundant with
  seg_label; the only net-new content is the pretrained language-space
  INTER-CLASS GEOMETRY. Assets: tools/gen_text_anchors.py -> frozen CLIP
  ViT-B/32 class-name anchors (offline, ~300KB; no text model in
  training/inference graphs).
- LTA (LCR + anchors as scorer class_embed via linear proj + 0.1 residual):
  46.95. PTA (plain PARSeg3 + anchored Offset_Learning cls_repr): 46.97.
  Both catastrophic (~-1.2 vs base).
- LTC (LTA + GT-routed class-granular InfoNCE, prototypes->anchors): 48.48
  ~= LCR band. QUASI-REPLICATION of the LCR-core level: {48.60, 48.48} vs
  all non-LCR-core runs {46.9..48.2}.
- Mechanism (ckpt forensics): CLIP class-name cone. LTA class-vector
  pairwise cos mean 0.628 / p95 0.810, residual/projection norm ratio 1.05
  (could not escape); LTC after InfoNCE: mean 0.013 / p95 0.285, ratio 3.04
  (InfoNCE = antidote that re-spreads the cone, then net text contribution
  ~ 0). Confusable pairs are CLOSE in text space (armchair~chair 0.96,
  sidewalk~road 0.90, door~wall 0.86): language relatedness ~ visual
  confusability, so raw name-embedding geometry is the WRONG-SIGN prior for
  class vectors.
- Conclusion: text-as-class-geometry is dead at both injection points.
  Open follow-ups (not run): (a) language similarity inverted into a
  confusability/hardness prior; (b) per-class discriminative DESCRIPTIONS
  (not names) grounding PAL attribute tokens.

## SAF (supervised arbitrated fusion): DELIVERED, running next

- Target: the un-cashed fusion oracle -- perfect per-pixel choice between
  the two heads = +1.9 (measured earlier; both opinions computed at
  inference, not a mirage-class oracle). Fusion is the one subsystem never
  attacked all year.
- Mechanism: AGCF replaced by a small arbiter (25.8k params) reading
  DETACHED per-head stats + 1x1-compressed logits; alpha in (0,1), final =
  base + alpha*(refine-base); NEW supervision = BCE(alpha, free meta-label
  "which head was right") on disagreement pixels only; alpha starts uniform
  0.12. Verified: BCE gradient reaches arbiter only (heads exactly 0).
- Files: PARSegSAF.py, parsegsaf_ade20k_160k-512x512.py,
  tools/saf_sanity_forward.py. From-scratch 160k.
- Read-out: success >= 48.7 at 160k; kill if 96k-128k clearly below try1
  curve. If it lands: v2 = role-specialize the heads toward the two error
  families (absent-FP vs present-conf) to GROW the disagreement oracle.

## Text follow-up analysis (no GPU): inversion idea measured and dropped

- "Language similarity as a confusability prior": measured on the top-25
  confusion pairs vs the name-anchor matrix. Confusion pairs' text cos
  mean 0.841 vs all-pairs background 0.782 (p90 0.847); within-row
  percentile mean 0.824; only 8/25 confusion targets in text top-5.
  Conclusion: name-level text is only weakly informative about visual
  confusability; the model's own logits localize rivals far better (that
  is LCR). Inversion idea dropped without a training slot.
- Remaining text hypothesis: descriptions, not names (attribute content).

## PAT (description-grounded PAL attribute tokens): DELIVERED, running next

- Mechanism: 150 x 6 hand-written attribute descriptions (material/shape/
  position/function/context/appearance; NO negation, NO explicit contrast
  -- CLIP encodes negation unreliably and explicit contrast would
  contaminate attribute content; owner decision). Frozen CLIP embeddings
  kept as per-class SETS [150,6,512], never averaged (averaging re-creates
  the cone). Training-only InfoNCE: present classes' calibrated attr
  tokens vs all classes' description sets, score = mean_a max_k cosine
  (soft assignment; tokens free to specialize). pat_w=0.15, tau=0.1,
  ramp 8k. Forward/inference = PARSeg3 exactly (verified bit-identical);
  new params = 512x256 projection only (+1.8MB frozen buffer in ckpt).
- Precedent for the bet: PALX's GT-anchoring of PAL geometry gave the
  year's rare positive spikes; PAT is the language version with a
  STATIONARY frozen-text target (also fixes SDR's drifting-teacher and
  LCR2's input-drift failure modes at this location).
- Files: tools/ade20k_class_descriptions.txt (SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for
  the text, plain data file, hand-editable; any uniform K per class),
  tools/gen_text_descriptions.py (offline encoder, parses the txt),
  PARSegPAT.py, parsegpat_ade20k_160k-512x512.py,
  tools/pat_sanity_forward.py. Re-run the encoder after any txt edit.
- Read-out vs base try1 48.17; kill 96k-128k if clearly below try1 curve.
  Portfolio framing: SAF = the mIoU bet (fusion subsystem), PAT = the
  narrative bet (PAL x language, one slot only).

## Text round 2 (three slots): LDR / TDL / TAM -- DELIVERED, running next

Three distinct text-usage principles, all reusing the desc6 asset, all
text-free at inference (frozen buffers only), all starting at or near the
plain PARSeg3 operating point:

- LDR (text as EVIDENCE): LCR v1 skeleton unchanged (learned class embed,
  gate, aux+rank losses); the scorer additionally sees three per-candidate
  description-match scalars (max/mean cos of pixel feature vs the
  candidate's 6 projected descriptions + gap to top-1). Augment-never-
  constrain (inverted LTA lesson); delta zero-init. Compare vs LCR band
  (48.60/48.48) to isolate the text-evidence effect.
- TDL (text as CONTENT): 900-entry frozen description dictionary; PAL
  refinement features retrieve from it by single-head cross-attention
  (q 256->64, bank k/v), added back through a bounded scalar gate
  (init 0.05, max 0.5). DTFormer/TSAM lineage made train/inference-
  symmetric. Base head untouched. Watch learned gate in ckpt; gate->0 =
  dictionary rejected. Compare vs try1 48.17.
  v2 (pre-launch, owner-prompted critique): CE-only retrieval supervision
  judged too indirect (predictable degeneration into a generic feature
  bias / random-basis layer). Added a GT-routed alignment loss (attention
  mass onto the GT class's own K entries; w=0.1, ramp 8k) + live needle
  `acc_tdl_gt_mass` (uniform baseline 1/150=0.0067) + `tdl_random_bank`
  flag for the text-content-load-bearing control run.
- TAM (text as METRIC): per-class diagonal channel weights on the
  refinement cosine, w_c = 1 + 0.5*tanh(W(desc_mean_c)+r_c), W/r zero-init
  -> starts EXACTLY PARSeg3 (verified bit-identical). Language seeds which
  channels matter per class; bounded positive weights carry no cross-class
  geometry constraint (cone cannot apply). Compare vs try1 48.17; check
  spread of w_c post-training.
- Verified locally (tools/text3_sanity_forward.py): identity/near-identity
  at init, all losses finite, gradients reach every text pathway; registry
  builds at 150 classes. New params: LDR ~101k / TDL ~180k / TAM ~169k.
- Kill rules: 96k-128k clearly below the respective reference curve.

## Results: PAT / SAF / LDR (2026-07 readouts)

- PAT (description-grounded PAL attribute tokens): 48.27 final.
  +0.10 over base try1 (48.17) = inside run noise; well under the LCR band
  (48.60/48.48). The PAL-grounding text bet is NEUTRAL, mirroring LTC:
  properly controlled text contributions keep landing at ~0.
- SAF (supervised arbitrated fusion): 47.9 final. FAILED (-0.27 vs base).
  The +1.9 fusion oracle did NOT cash. Candidate causes, honestly ranked:
  (a) "who-is-right" is learnable on train but generalizes poorly to val
  (predicting rightness ~ predicting errors = hard); AGCF's entropy
  heuristics generalize better than a trained meta-classifier;
  (b) recipe side effect: AGCF's gate computation is NOT detached in
  PARSeg3 (its entropy path feeds gradient into both heads); SAF detached
  the arbiter inputs, silently removing a head-shaping pathway;
  (c) arbiter overfit to train disagreement statistics.
  Decision: fusion-redesign line shelved. v2 (head specialization) is moot
  without a working arbiter.
- LDR (description evidence in the LCR scorer): killed at 88k -- two val
  points significantly below the same-iter values of the reference logs.
  Kill rule applied AT THE GATE this time (process win). Contrast with LTC
  (neutral): appending desc-match features to the scorer's input hurts,
  while anchor+InfoNCE was merely neutral -- co-trained desc-match maps
  likely fed unstable evidence into the candidate corrections during the
  formative phase.
- Running/pending at this writing: TAM, TDL(v2). Text scorecard so far:
  LTA -1.2 / PTA -1.2 / LTC ~0 / PAT ~0 / LDR negative(killed);
  zero real text wins through five completed experiments.

## ACR (auto-context re-decision): DELIVERED, running next

- The untried subsystem-level move: a second decision round that sees the
  SPATIAL ARRANGEMENT of round-1's beliefs. Layout encoder (1x1 class
  embedding -> 3x3 d=1 -> 3x3 d=4) over softmax(final_r1).DETACHED;
  round 2 = second Offset_Learning over fuse([feat_aligned, layout]);
  output = final_r1 + g*(r2 - final_r1), g learned scalar in (0,1),
  init 0.1 (may interpolate to "round 2 is the decision maker").
- Targets: confident REGIONAL collapses among layout classes (the
  diagnosed interior present-conf mass). Spatial presence was never
  tested; global presence's realizable-0 does not cover it.
- Recipe purity (owner attribution demand): round-1 losses byte-identical
  to PARSeg3; only additions are CE(r2)=1.0 and anchor CE(final_r1)=0.5.
  Recipe-layer levers (aux heads / crop / schedule) are permanently
  rejected as unattributable -- all gains must be structural.
- Verified: recipe loss set intact + 2 ACR terms finite; r2-CE gradient
  reaches layout/r2 modules with EXACTLY zero leakage into round-1-
  exclusive modules (AGCF/PAL) through the detached layout channel;
  gate init 0.1; 150-class registry build. New params ~336k.
- Live monitoring: `acc_acr_gate` (rising = round 2 earning trust; stuck
  at 0.1 = layout adds nothing). Read-out vs base try1 48.17. Kill:
  96k-128k clearly below try1 curve.

## ACT / TAX (two slots, owner respec): text models WITH aux heads

Owner replaced the plain factorial round: the two slots must be NEW models
that involve text AND carry the auxiliary head. Delivered:

- ACT (text into the INFERENCE structure): PARSegACR whose layout encoder
  mixes round-1's belief map through description-anchored class weights
  (text_proj(desc_mean) + zero-init free residual) -- the second round
  reads the scene as a SEMANTIC COMPOSITION. First site where the language
  cone HELPS by design (layout reasoning wants related classes to yield
  related layout features; the cone only kills discrimination roles).
  Plus standard FCN aux head. NOTE vs plain ACR the diff is text-layout
  AND aux (disclosed in config); vs base it is structure-only.
- TAX (text into the TRAINING structure): stage-3 auxiliary classifier =
  cosine head over language-derived class vectors (W@desc_mean + UNbounded
  zero-init residual, tau 0.1). Pulls backbone mid-features toward the
  language class geometry; the whole head is discarded at inference --
  deployed model = pure PARSeg3. Cone failure cannot recur by construction
  (text vectors only carry a disposable auxiliary decision; residual can
  escape). vs base+aux (if ever run) isolates the LANGUAGE part.
- Verified: ACT text-layout grads flow (proj/residual), recipe loss set
  intact, eval OK; TAX logits bounded by 1/tau, grads reach proj/residual/
  convs, framework discards aux at inference; 150-class builds for both.
- Kill rules: 96k-128k clearly below try1 curve.

